Object.assign(window.deepArticles,{
  "republic-of-german-austria":firstRepublicFoundation({
    title:"Republic of German-Austria",category:"Former states",eyebrow:"Republik Deutschösterreich · 1918–1919",infoboxKicker:"Immediate successor to Habsburg Austria",landscape:germanAustriaProclamation,
    lead:"The Republic of German-Austria was the state formed by the German-speaking representatives of the collapsing Habsburg monarchy in October and November 1918. It claimed the German-majority districts of former Cisleithania, proclaimed a democratic republic on 12 November, introduced universal suffrage, and declared itself part of the German Republic. The Treaty of Saint-Germain rejected both its territorial program and immediate union, compelling the name Republic of Austria in October 1919.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-o3g8u writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:40:00-06:00 -->The state’s complete existence, institutions, territorial claims, declaration of union, and replacement by the Republic of Austria are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The distinction between democratic German union in 1918 and coercive National Socialist annexation in 1938 is historical and constitutional analysis, not a post-divergence claim.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"collapse",title:"Collapse of the Habsburg state",html:"<p>Military defeat and national secession dissolved the common institutions of Austria-Hungary during October 1918. German-speaking members of the imperial House of Deputies met on 21 October as the Provisional National Assembly for German-Austria.</p><p>On 30 October the assembly adopted a provisional constitution and formed a State Council and government under <a href='karl-renner.html'>Karl Renner</a>. Ministries, food supply, demobilization, railways, currency, and public employment had to be separated from an imperial system that was disappearing around them.</p>"},
      {id:"territory",title:"The territorial claim",html:"<p>German-Austria claimed German-majority districts across the former Austrian half of the monarchy, not merely the borders of the later republic. The claim included German Bohemia and the Sudeten districts, South Tyrol, and German-speaking enclaves and border areas in Carinthia, Styria, and elsewhere.</p><p>Czechoslovak, Italian, and South Slav forces or administrations controlled much of this territory. The map on paper therefore exceeded the land the government in Vienna could govern.</p>"},
      {id:"republic",title:"Proclamation on 12 November",html:"<p>Emperor Karl withdrew from participation in state affairs on 11 November without formally abdicating. The assembly proclaimed German-Austria a democratic republic the following day before a vast crowd outside Parliament.</p><p>The founding law also declared German-Austria a component of the German Republic. Social Democrats regarded union as national self-determination and economic necessity; many Christian Social and German-national politicians reached the same conclusion through different ideological routes.</p>"},
      {id:"institutions",title:"Provisional institutions",html:"<p>The assembly, State Council, State Chancellery, and departmental state offices converted inherited administrative personnel into republican government. <a href='karl-seitz.html'>Karl Seitz</a>, Franz Dinghofer, and Jodok Fink shared the assembly’s presidency and represented the three principal political camps.</p><p>Provisional law established equal, direct, secret, and universal suffrage for men and women. The <a href='1919-austrian-constituent-assembly-election.html'>February 1919 election</a> replaced the assembly of former imperial deputies with a body chosen by the new electorate.</p>"},
      {id:"coalition",title:"The Renner coalition",html:"<p>Social Democrats and Christian Socials governed together through demobilization, food emergency, labor unrest, and border conflict. The coalition enacted the eight-hour day, works councils, unemployment measures, and other social reforms while preventing the most radical councils and guards from replacing parliamentary authority.</p><p>Its cooperation did not eliminate the parties’ incompatible social projects. The founding settlement was an emergency partnership rather than a durable fusion.</p>"},
      {id:"peace",title:"Saint-Germain and the end of the name",html:"<p>The Allied powers refused to recognize the wider territorial claim or union with Germany. The <a href='treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye.html'>Treaty of Saint-Germain</a> fixed the smaller state’s borders, required the name Republic of Austria, and made union dependent upon international consent.</p><p>The Constituent National Assembly approved the treaty under protest and enacted the new name on 21 October 1919. German-Austria’s institutions continued inside the <a href='first-austrian-republic.html'>Republic of Austria</a>, but its declared national and territorial program had ended.</p>"},
      {id:"memory",title:"Memory under Greater German rule",html:"<p>After the victorious Reich makes annexation permanent, official history can portray 1918 as proof that Austria always sought incorporation. That reading removes the republican, parliamentary, and self-determining terms under which union was proposed.</p><p>Regional archives preserve German-Austria as both a failed national claim and the institutional beginning of Austrian democracy.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"first-austrian-republic.html",kicker:"Institutional successor",label:"First Austrian Republic"},{href:"treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye.html",kicker:"Peace settlement",label:"Treaty of Saint-Germain"},{href:"1919-austrian-constituent-assembly-election.html",kicker:"First general election",label:"1919 Constituent Assembly Election"},{href:"karl-renner.html",kicker:"State Chancellor",label:"Karl Renner"},{href:"karl-seitz.html",kicker:"Assembly president",label:"Karl Seitz"},{href:"anschluss-1938.html",kicker:"Later coercive annexation",label:"Anschluss of 1938"}],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/verstehen/historisches/1918-1945/geburt-der-republik",label:"Austrian Parliament — birth of the republic"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/verstehen/historisches/1918-1945/ausrufung-republik/index.html",label:"Austrian Parliament — proclamation on 12 November 1918"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ausrufung_der_Republik_Deutsch%C3%B6sterreich_1918_Parlamentsrampe.jpg",label:"Wikimedia Commons — proclamation photograph and rights record"}],
    facts:[["Provisional assembly","21 October 1918"],["Provisional constitution","30 October 1918"],["Republic proclaimed","12 November 1918"],["Capital","Vienna"],["State Chancellor","Karl Renner"],["Legislature","Provisional, then Constituent National Assembly"],["Declared objective","Democratic union with Germany"],["Successor name","Republic of Austria · 21 October 1919"],["Setting interpretation","Republican union distinct from the 1938 Anschluss"]]
  }),

  "treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye":firstRepublicFoundation({
    title:"Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye",category:"Treaties",eyebrow:"Paris peace settlement · 10 September 1919",infoboxKicker:"Peace treaty between Austria and the Allied powers",landscape:saintGermainDelegation,
    lead:"The Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye was the peace treaty signed between the Republic of German-Austria and the Allied and Associated Powers on 10 September 1919. It recognized the dissolution of Austria-Hungary, fixed the borders of the smaller Austrian republic, required the name Austria, restricted its armed forces, and prohibited union with Germany without international consent. It entered into force on 16 July 1920.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p4h9v writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:45:00-06:00 -->The treaty, its provisions, territorial settlement, military restrictions, union prohibition, and implementation are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Later Reich denunciation of it as a victors’ dictate remains political interpretation; the treaty’s established provisions are distinct from post-victory uses made of them.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"conference",title:"Austria at the peace conference",html:"<p>The victorious powers treated the Habsburg monarchy as dissolved before the Austrian delegation arrived. <a href='karl-renner.html'>Karl Renner</a> led a delegation permitted to submit observations but not to negotiate as an equal participant in the main conference.</p><p>Austrian arguments for economic viability, German-speaking border districts, and union with Germany changed few central terms. The delegation signed under the pressure of continued blockade, food shortage, and diplomatic isolation.</p>"},
      {id:"succession",title:"End of the Habsburg international order",html:"<p>The treaty recognized Czechoslovakia, the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, Poland, and Hungary as separate states or successor authorities. Austria renounced claims to territories assigned to them and to Italy.</p><p>The settlement did not divide one coherent nation-state. It converted a multinational imperial space into several states whose new borders still enclosed linguistic and regional minorities.</p>"},
      {id:"borders",title:"Borders and territorial losses",html:"<p>Bohemia, Moravia, Austrian Silesia, and German-majority border districts entered Czechoslovakia. South Tyrol, Trentino, and former Austrian Adriatic territories went to Italy; Lower Styria and Carniola entered the South Slav kingdom.</p><p>The treaty promised western Hungarian territory to Austria, producing the later <a href='burgenland-dispute.html'>Burgenland dispute</a>. A separate plebiscite confirmed southern Carinthia in Austria.</p>"},
      {id:"name",title:"Austria and the Anschluss prohibition",html:"<p>The state could no longer use the name German-Austria. Union with Germany required the consent of the League of Nations, preventing the immediate implementation of the founding republic’s Article 2.</p><p>The prohibition addressed the European balance of power as much as Austrian constitutional preference. It later became a central grievance for democratic pan-Germans and National Socialists alike, although they sought radically different political orders.</p>"},
      {id:"military",title:"Military and financial restrictions",html:"<p>The Austrian army was limited to thirty thousand long-service volunteers. Conscription, a general staff, and major categories of heavy armament were prohibited; the imperial navy had already ceased to exist as an Austrian force.</p><p>The treaty imposed reparations liability and controls over state property, archives, cultural claims, and succession obligations. Austria’s inability to pay on the expected scale did not remove the uncertainty these provisions created.</p>"},
      {id:"ratification",title:"Ratification and implementation",html:"<p>The Constituent National Assembly approved the treaty on 17 October 1919 under explicit protest. A law of 21 October adopted the name Republic of Austria. Ratification followed, and the treaty entered into force on 16 July 1920.</p><p>Boundary commissions then translated broad treaty lines into surveyed frontiers. The settlement therefore remained an administrative process after the diplomatic signature.</p>"},
      {id:"consequences",title:"Political consequences",html:"<p>The treaty left Vienna at the center of a state much smaller than the economy and bureaucracy built around it. Questions of food supply, coal, markets, transit, public employment, and currency reinforced doubt about viability and helped produce the <a href='geneva-protocols-1922.html'>Geneva stabilization</a>.</p><p>Every major camp could use Saint-Germain as evidence: Social Democrats for international injustice, Christian Socials for a sovereign Austrian mission, German nationalists for revision, and later National Socialists for the destruction of the postwar order.</p>"},
      {id:"setting",title:"Legacy after permanent annexation",html:"<p>German victory abolishes the treaty’s anti-union settlement in practice. Official commemoration presents that outcome as historical correction.</p><p>The treaty nevertheless remains the legal origin of borders, citizenship questions, archives, and minority disputes that the Reich inherits rather than erases.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"republic-of-german-austria.html",kicker:"State constrained",label:"Republic of German-Austria"},{href:"first-austrian-republic.html",kicker:"Resulting republic",label:"First Austrian Republic"},{href:"burgenland-dispute.html",kicker:"Territorial consequence",label:"Burgenland Dispute"},{href:"karl-renner.html",kicker:"Austrian signatory",label:"Karl Renner"},{href:"geneva-protocols-1922.html",kicker:"Financial aftermath",label:"Geneva Protocols"},{href:"anschluss-1938.html",kicker:"Later destruction",label:"Anschluss of 1938"}],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/verstehen/historisches/1918-1945/parlament-im-zentrum/index.html",label:"Austrian Parliament — the peace settlement and founding parliament"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/dokument/KN/KNSITZ/102/fnameorig_816000.html",label:"Austrian Parliament — contemporary account of approval and implementation"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/verstehen/historisches/1918-1945/geburt-der-republik",label:"Austrian Parliament — the founding republic and Saint-Germain"}],
    facts:[["Signed","10 September 1919"],["Location","Saint-Germain-en-Laye · France"],["Austrian delegation","Led by Karl Renner"],["Assembly approval","17 October 1919"],["Entered into force","16 July 1920"],["Required state name","Republic of Austria"],["Army limit","30,000 volunteers"],["Union with Germany","Required international consent"],["Principal territorial effect","Recognition of the post-Habsburg borders"]]
  }),

  "1919-austrian-constituent-assembly-election":firstRepublicFoundation({
    title:"1919 Austrian Constituent Assembly election",category:"Elections",eyebrow:"Deutschösterreich · 16 February 1919",infoboxKicker:"First election under universal suffrage",landscape:womenVoting1919,
    lead:"The 1919 Austrian Constituent Assembly election was held on 16 February to choose the parliament that would give the new republic a permanent constitution. It was the first Austrian national election in which women could vote and stand as candidates. The Social Democratic Workers’ Party emerged as the largest party with 72 of the eventual 170 seats, followed by the Christian Socials with 69, and the two formed a grand coalition.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-q5i1w writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:50:00-06:00 -->The election, franchise, campaign, result, coalition consequences, territorial voting limits, and exclusion of registered prostitutes are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The article’s qualification of “universal suffrage” records the historical limits of an otherwise transformative franchise.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"franchise",title:"A new electorate",html:"<p>The founding law of 12 November 1918 established universal, equal, direct, and secret suffrage. The Provisional National Assembly adopted the election law on 18 December.</p><p>Women voted and stood for national office for the first time regardless of property or education. Registered prostitutes remained excluded, a restriction removed in 1923.</p>"},
      {id:"territory",title:"An election across disputed borders",html:"<p>The <a href='republic-of-german-austria.html'>German-Austrian</a> state claimed districts already occupied by Czechoslovakia, Italy, and South Slav forces. Voting could occur only in limited portions of several claimed areas.</p><p>Eleven additional deputies were later called to represent occupied territories, bringing the assembly from 159 elected members to 170. The procedure preserved the territorial claim without creating control on the ground.</p>"},
      {id:"campaign",title:"Parties and campaign",html:"<p>The <a href='social-democratic-workers-party-austria.html'>Social Democrats</a> campaigned as the party of the republic, labor reform, and democratic union with Germany. The <a href='christian-social-party-austria.html'>Christian Socials</a> mobilized Catholic, rural, provincial, and middle-class networks. German-national lists competed for pan-German and liberal voters.</p><p>Food shortage, demobilization, social revolution, church authority, property, and national borders gave constitutional questions immediate material meaning.</p>"},
      {id:"result",title:"Result",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Seats in the eventual 170-member assembly</th><th>Position</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Social Democratic Workers’ Party</td><td>72</td><td>Largest party</td></tr><tr><td>Christian Social Party and allies</td><td>69</td><td>Second party</td></tr><tr><td>German-national parties</td><td>27</td><td>Several lists grouped by Parliament statistics</td></tr><tr><td>Jewish National Party</td><td>1</td><td>Minority representation</td></tr><tr><td>Other</td><td>1</td><td>Small-party mandate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Turnout was about 84.5 percent. Women participated at a rate above 82 percent and men at nearly 87 percent.</p>"},
      {id:"women",title:"Women enter Parliament",html:"<p>Eight women took seats: seven Social Democrats—Anna Boschek, Emmy Freundlich, Adelheid Popp, Gabriele Proft, Therese Schlesinger, Amalie Seidel, and Maria Tusch—and Christian Social Hildegard Burjan.</p><p>Their presence made political equality visible without producing equal representation. Women formed a majority of eligible voters but fewer than five percent of deputies.</p>"},
      {id:"government",title:"Grand coalition",html:"<p>Neither mass party could govern alone, and the emergency favored cooperation. Renner remained State Chancellor while Christian Social leader Jodok Fink became vice chancellor.</p><p>The coalition enacted social reforms, managed the peace settlement, and drafted the <a href='federal-constitutional-law-1920.html'>1920 constitution</a>. It also hardened the two camps by giving each a national organization, electorate, and claim to have founded the state.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Democratic legacy",html:"<p>The election transformed the provisional republic into a state based on mass suffrage. Its boundaries and exclusions were real, but so was the transfer of authority from imperial deputies to voters.</p><p>In the setting, permanent annexation prevents a later Austrian republic from claiming direct institutional continuity. The 1919 vote survives as the first and most inclusive founding election of the extinguished state.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"republic-of-german-austria.html",kicker:"State holding election",label:"Republic of German-Austria"},{href:"social-democratic-workers-party-austria.html",kicker:"Largest party",label:"Social Democratic Workers’ Party"},{href:"christian-social-party-austria.html",kicker:"Coalition partner",label:"Christian Social Party"},{href:"federal-constitutional-law-1920.html",kicker:"Assembly’s principal work",label:"Federal Constitutional Law"},{href:"1920-austrian-legislative-election.html",kicker:"Following election",label:"1920 Legislative Election"},{href:"national-council-first-republic.html",kicker:"Successor chamber",label:"National Council"}],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/aktuelles/news/16.-Februar-1919-Der-erste-Wahlsonntag-fuer-Frauen",label:"Austrian Parliament — first national election with women voting"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/verstehen/demokratie-wahlen/frauen-im-parlament/wahlrecht",label:"Austrian Parliament — women’s suffrage and turnout"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/recherchieren/statistiken/personen-statistiken/zusammensetzung-nr/index.html",label:"Austrian Parliament — composition of the Constituent National Assembly"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Women_voting_in_Wieden,_Vienna_1919.jpg",label:"Wikimedia Commons — Wieden polling-station photograph and rights record"}],
    facts:[["Election date","16 February 1919"],["Turnout","About 84.5%"],["Seats","170 eventual members · 159 elected and 11 later called"],["Largest party","Social Democratic Workers’ Party · 72 seats"],["Second party","Christian Social Party · 69 seats"],["Historical distinction","First Austrian national election with women voting and standing"],["Women elected","8"],["Government formed","Social Democratic–Christian Social coalition"],["Assembly first met","4 March 1919"]]
  }),

  "1920-austrian-legislative-election":firstRepublicFoundation({
    title:"1920 Austrian legislative election",category:"Elections",eyebrow:"First National Council election · 17 October 1920",infoboxKicker:"Election ending the founding grand coalition",landscape:christianSocial1920,
    lead:"The 1920 Austrian legislative election was the first election to the National Council under the Federal Constitutional Law. Held principally on 17 October, it made the Christian Social Party the largest parliamentary force with 85 of 183 seats and reduced the Social Democrats to 69. The result ended the founding grand coalition and began twelve years of Christian Social-led federal government.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-r6j2x writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T03:55:00-06:00 -->The election, campaign, delayed territorial votes, result, and eventual chamber composition are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Its antisemitic campaigning remains part of the historical political record and cannot be detached from Christian Social governance and Catholic social policy.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"background",title:"End of the founding coalition",html:"<p>The 1919 coalition had enacted social reform, accepted Saint-Germain, and negotiated the constitution. Peace and constitutional settlement removed part of the emergency that had required cooperation.</p><p>Social conflict, confessional division, Vienna’s radical image, and disagreement over economic policy made both mass parties prefer an election to continued shared responsibility.</p>"},
      {id:"system",title:"The first National Council contest",html:"<p>The <a href='federal-constitutional-law-1920.html'>Federal Constitutional Law</a> created a directly elected National Council and a provincial Federal Council. Proportional representation preserved several political camps while making coalition government necessary.</p><p>The principal election occurred on 17 October. Voting in Carinthia and the newly acquired Burgenland followed later as territorial administration was settled.</p>"},
      {id:"campaign",title:"Campaign and political language",html:"<p>Christian Socials defended Catholic society, provincial interests, private property, and order against “Red Vienna.” Their propaganda frequently used explicit antisemitism, representing Jewish figures as the hidden agents of finance, liberalism, and socialism.</p><p>Social Democrats defended labor legislation, secular government, municipal reform, and the founding republic. German-national groups advocated union with Germany, liberal or nationalist economics, and opposition to both clericalism and Marxism.</p>"},
      {id:"result",title:"Result",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Seats</th><th>Approximate vote share</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Christian Social Party</td><td>85</td><td>41.8%</td></tr><tr><td>Social Democratic Workers’ Party</td><td>69</td><td>36.0%</td></tr><tr><td>German-national parties</td><td>28</td><td>17.3%</td></tr><tr><td>Civic Workers’ Party</td><td>1</td><td>Small-party mandate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Turnout was about 80.3 percent. No party held the 92 seats required for an absolute majority in the eventual 183-member chamber.</p>"},
      {id:"government",title:"Christian Social-led government",html:"<p>Christian Social Michael Mayr remained chancellor and formed a government supported by German-national deputies. The arrangement replaced the grand coalition with a bourgeois bloc whose composition varied but whose exclusion of Social Democracy became durable.</p><p>Federal government and socialist Vienna now represented rival governing systems inside the same republic.</p>"},
      {id:"consequence",title:"Long constitutional consequence",html:"<p>Social Democrats would not return to federal cabinet before their prohibition in 1934. They retained electoral strength and built Red Vienna while Christian Social-led coalitions controlled federal ministries, appointments, and security policy.</p><p>The election therefore created the central institutional asymmetry of the First Republic: one camp governed the capital and became the largest party again by 1930, while another governed the federal state through coalition.</p>"},
      {id:"memory",title:"The election in later memory",html:"<p>Authoritarian and German accounts use the fragmentation of 1920 to depict parliament as camp rule. The result also shows the opposite: a mass electorate transferred power without civil conflict and opposition retained legal municipal government.</p><p>That constitutional possibility ended through executive action in 1933, not through the election itself.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"1919-austrian-constituent-assembly-election.html",kicker:"Previous election",label:"1919 Constituent Assembly Election"},{href:"christian-social-party-austria.html",kicker:"Largest party",label:"Christian Social Party"},{href:"social-democratic-workers-party-austria.html",kicker:"Federal opposition",label:"Social Democratic Workers’ Party"},{href:"national-council-first-republic.html",kicker:"Chamber elected",label:"National Council"},{href:"federal-constitutional-law-1920.html",kicker:"New constitutional framework",label:"Federal Constitutional Law"},{href:"1930-austrian-legislative-election.html",kicker:"Last free election",label:"1930 Legislative Election"}],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/recherchieren/statistiken/personen-statistiken/zusammensetzung-nr/index.html",label:"Austrian Parliament — composition of the First National Council"},{href:"https://www.dasrotewien.at/seite/konstituierende-nationalversammlung",label:"Das Rote Wien — election result and transition to the National Council"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Deutsche_Christen_Rettet_Osterreich%22_(Vote_Christian_Socialist%3B_German_Christians_will_save_Austria)_by_Bernd_Steiner_(1920)_HQ_Ed.png",label:"Wikimedia Commons — Christian Social campaign poster and rights record"}],
    facts:[["Principal election date","17 October 1920"],["Turnout","About 80.3%"],["Seats","183"],["Largest party","Christian Social Party · 85"],["Second party","Social Democratic Workers’ Party · 69"],["German-national parties","28 seats"],["Government","Christian Social-led bourgeois coalition"],["Historical consequence","End of the founding grand coalition"],["Next general election","21 October 1923"]]
  }),

  "1930-austrian-legislative-election":firstRepublicFoundation({
    title:"1930 Austrian legislative election",category:"Elections",eyebrow:"Fourth National Council election · 9 November 1930",infoboxKicker:"Last free parliamentary election of the First Republic",landscape:nationalCouncil1930,
    lead:"The 1930 Austrian legislative election was the fourth and last free election to the National Council during the First Republic. The Social Democratic Workers’ Party became the largest party with 72 of 165 seats, ahead of the Christian Social-led lists with 66. It could not assemble a governing majority, and a conservative coalition under Otto Ender remained in office while parliamentary government approached its final crisis.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s7k3y writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T04:00:00-06:00 -->The election, campaign, result, party competition, and parliament it created are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> German victory and the non-restoration of Austria are direct setting canon, making this the last free Austrian national election in the setting as well as in interwar history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"dissolution",title:"Presidential dissolution",html:"<p>President Wilhelm Miklas dissolved the National Council on 1 October 1930 at the government’s request, the only use of the strengthened presidential dissolution power during the First Republic. The election followed on 9 November.</p><p>Economic strain, Heimwehr pressure, constitutional revision, and competition among conservative camps shaped a contest held before the full domestic impact of the Creditanstalt crisis.</p>"},
      {id:"lists",title:"Parties and electoral blocs",html:"<p>Social Democrats entered as a disciplined national mass party and the government of Red Vienna. Christian Socials formed local alliances with Heimwehr organizations in Vienna, Lower Austria, and parts of Burgenland.</p><p>Johannes Schober joined Greater Germans, the Landbund, and economic groups in the National Economic Bloc and Landbund. The Heimwehr also contested independently through the Heimatblock under <a href='ernst-rudiger-starhemberg.html'>Ernst Rüdiger Starhemberg</a>.</p>"},
      {id:"result",title:"Result",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Seats</th><th>Approximate vote share</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Social Democratic Workers’ Party</td><td>72</td><td>41%</td></tr><tr><td>Christian Social Party and allied lists</td><td>66</td><td>36%</td></tr><tr><td>National Economic Bloc and Landbund</td><td>19</td><td>About 12%</td></tr><tr><td>Heimatblock</td><td>8</td><td>About 6%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Turnout was about ninety percent. National Socialists received roughly three percent and no National Council seat.</p>"},
      {id:"largest",title:"A largest party without government",html:"<p>Social Democracy’s 72 seats made it the largest single parliamentary group but left it eleven seats short of a majority. Christian Socials, national-economic deputies, and Heimatblock members together could sustain a government to its right.</p><p>Otto Ender became chancellor in December. The result demonstrated that proportional plurality and cabinet control were different achievements.</p>"},
      {id:"depression",title:"The parliament of the depression",html:"<p>The <a href='creditanstalt-collapse.html'>Creditanstalt collapse</a> in May 1931 transformed the economic environment. Banking rescue, exchange controls, austerity, and mass unemployment narrowed the coalition’s choices and discredited parliamentary bargaining.</p><p>The <a href='pfrimer-putsch.html'>Pfrimer Putsch</a>, Nazi growth outside Parliament, and armed-camp politics placed pressure on institutions whose electoral mandate remained intact.</p>"},
      {id:"end",title:"From election to parliamentary extinction",html:"<p>Engelbert Dollfuss formed a one-vote coalition government in May 1932. On 4 March 1933 a disputed procedural vote led all three National Council presidents to resign their chairs temporarily.</p><p>Dollfuss claimed the chamber had eliminated itself and used police to prevent reconvening on 15 March. The elected legislature was disabled by executive force rather than defeated in another election.</p>"},
      {id:"last",title:"The last free Austrian national election",html:"<p>The corporate state abolished competitive national elections, and the Anschluss abolished Austrian statehood. In real history a restored republic voted again in 1945; that restoration does not occur here.</p><p>The 1930 electorate therefore remains the last body to choose a sovereign Austrian national parliament in the setting.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"national-council-first-republic.html",kicker:"Chamber elected",label:"National Council"},{href:"social-democratic-workers-party-austria.html",kicker:"Largest party",label:"Social Democratic Workers’ Party"},{href:"christian-social-party-austria.html",kicker:"Government core",label:"Christian Social Party"},{href:"heimwehr.html",kicker:"Heimatblock movement",label:"Heimwehr"},{href:"creditanstalt-collapse.html",kicker:"Economic crisis",label:"Creditanstalt Collapse"},{href:"austrian-civil-war.html",kicker:"Later destruction",label:"Austrian Civil War"}],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/recherchieren/statistiken/personen-statistiken/zusammensetzung-nr/index.html",label:"Austrian Parliament — 1930 National Council composition and legislative period"},{href:"https://www.mediathek.at/onlineausstellungen/der-erste-weltkrieg/der-erste-weltkrieg-ausgabe-6/das-ende-der-1-republik/das-ende-der-ersten-republik",label:"Austrian Mediathek — the last free election and end of the First Republic"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1930-parliament-election-in-austria.svg",label:"Wikimedia Commons — 1930 chamber diagram and rights record"}],
    facts:[["Election date","9 November 1930"],["Turnout","About 90%"],["Seats","165"],["Largest party","Social Democratic Workers’ Party · 72"],["Christian Social and allied lists","66 seats"],["National Economic Bloc and Landbund","19 seats"],["Heimatblock","8 seats"],["Government formed","Conservative coalition under Otto Ender"],["Historical distinction","Last free national election of sovereign Austria"]]
  }),

  "burgenland-dispute":firstRepublicFoundation({
    title:"Burgenland dispute",category:"Territorial disputes",eyebrow:"Austria–Hungary border crisis · 1918–1921",infoboxKicker:"Transfer of German West Hungary to Austria",landscape:sopronPlebisciteMap,
    lead:"The Burgenland dispute was the Austrian–Hungarian conflict over German West Hungary after the First World War. The peace treaties awarded most of the German- and Croatian-speaking border region to Austria, but Hungarian irregulars resisted the scheduled transfer in August 1921. Italian mediation produced the Venice Protocol, and a December plebiscite kept Sopron and eight surrounding communities in Hungary while most of Burgenland entered Austria.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t8l4z writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T04:05:00-06:00 -->The territorial settlement, irregular fighting, Venice Protocol, Sopron plebiscite, and establishment of Burgenland are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> German victory and the non-restoration of an Austrian federal state are direct setting canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Burgenland’s exact later administrative and commemorative position inside Greater Germany remains open unless separately established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"west-hungary",title:"German West Hungary",html:"<p>The territory that became Burgenland belonged to the Hungarian half of the Habsburg monarchy. Most inhabitants were German- or Croatian-speaking farmers and workers with economic ties to Lower Austria and Styria, while Hungarian administration and landholding remained important.</p><p>Imperial collapse turned ordinary movement across an internal frontier into a question of citizenship, customs, schools, railways, church jurisdiction, and national allegiance.</p>"},
      {id:"award",title:"The Paris peace award",html:"<p>The <a href='treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye.html'>Treaty of Saint-Germain</a> assigned the border strip to Austria, subject to the settlement with Hungary. The Treaty of Trianon confirmed Hungary’s obligation to transfer it.</p><p>Austrian campaigners invoked ethnic self-determination and economic connection. Hungary disputed the demographic case, the loss of historic counties, and the transfer of Sopron—Ödenburg—which Austria expected to make the new province’s capital.</p>"},
      {id:"resistance",title:"Irregular resistance in 1921",html:"<p>The handover was scheduled for 28 August 1921. Hungarian irregulars and nationalist volunteers resisted Austrian police and gendarmerie entering the territory, while Budapest denied or blurred direct responsibility.</p><p>For several weeks Pál Prónay’s followers proclaimed the short-lived Banate of Leitha. The entity lacked stable international recognition but demonstrated that the frontier could not be transferred by administrative order alone.</p>"},
      {id:"venice",title:"The Venice Protocol",html:"<p>Italian mediation produced the Venice Protocol on 13 October. Hungary accepted the transfer of most of the territory, and Austria accepted a plebiscite in Sopron and eight neighboring communities.</p><p>The compromise separated the general territorial award from the future of its expected capital and reduced the risk of formal interstate war.</p>"},
      {id:"plebiscite",title:"The Sopron plebiscite",html:"<p>Voting took place in December 1921. The city of Sopron strongly favored remaining in Hungary, while several surrounding villages voted for Austria; the combined district returned a Hungarian majority.</p><p>Austria criticized the conditions and Hungarian influence but accepted the result. Sopron remained Hungarian, and the new province had to construct an administrative center elsewhere.</p>"},
      {id:"integration",title:"Integration into Austria",html:"<p>Most of Burgenland entered Austrian sovereignty in November and December 1921 and became a self-governing federal Land. Legal systems, schools, police, railways, churches, and land administration required gradual transition from Hungarian to Austrian institutions.</p><p>The first provincial election occurred on 18 June 1922. After several provisional arrangements, Eisenstadt became the seat of the provincial government in 1925.</p>"},
      {id:"setting",title:"Burgenland under permanent German rule",html:"<p>The National Socialist government dissolved Burgenland as an administrative unit in 1938 and divided it between Niederdonau and Styria. German victory prevents the postwar restoration that occurred in real history.</p><p>By 1985 the Burgenland identity survives through border communities, dialect, Catholic and Protestant institutions, Croatian minority life, provincial memory, and the unresolved symbolism of Sopron across the frontier.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye.html",kicker:"Territorial award",label:"Treaty of Saint-Germain"},{href:"first-austrian-republic.html",kicker:"Receiving state",label:"First Austrian Republic"},{href:"schattendorf-incident.html",kicker:"Later provincial crisis",label:"Schattendorf Incident"},{href:"austria.html",kicker:"Later regional setting",label:"Austria within Greater Germany"}],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.burgenland.at/verwaltung/land-burgenland/geschichte/",label:"Land Burgenland — creation of Burgenland, 1918–1921"},{href:"https://www.burgenland.at/verwaltung/land-burgenland/geschichte/1922-1934/",label:"Land Burgenland — interwar administration and provincial politics"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sopron_Plebiscite_Map.svg",label:"Wikimedia Commons — Sopron plebiscite map and rights record"}],
    facts:[["Period","1918–1921"],["Territory","German West Hungary"],["Peace settlements","Saint-Germain and Trianon"],["Scheduled transfer","28 August 1921"],["Mediated agreement","Venice Protocol · 13 October 1921"],["Plebiscite","Sopron and eight communities · December 1921"],["Plebiscite result","Remain in Hungary"],["Most territory transferred","November–December 1921"],["First Landtag election","18 June 1922"],["Administrative seat","Eisenstadt from 1925"]]
  }),

  "national-council-first-republic":firstRepublicFoundation({
    title:"National Council of the First Austrian Republic",category:"Legislatures",eyebrow:"Nationalrat · 1920–1933",infoboxKicker:"Directly elected chamber of the Austrian Parliament",landscape:parliamentBuildingHistoric,
    lead:"The National Council was the directly elected lower chamber and principal legislative body of the First Austrian Republic. Created by the Federal Constitutional Law of 1920, it made and removed governments, enacted federal law and budgets, and represented Austria through proportional party competition. Its work ended after the resignation of its three presidents during a disputed vote on 4 March 1933 gave Chancellor Engelbert Dollfuss the pretext to block reconvening.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-u9m5a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T04:10:00-06:00 -->The chamber’s creation, elections, procedures, legislative periods, March 1933 paralysis, and final destruction are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> German victory and the absence of the historical 1945 restoration are direct setting canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Describing 1933–34 as institutional extinction rather than an interruption follows from that setting outcome while remaining an interpretive framing.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"creation",title:"Creation under the 1920 constitution",html:"<p>The <a href='federal-constitutional-law-1920.html'>Federal Constitutional Law</a> replaced the Constituent National Assembly with a bicameral Parliament. The National Council first met on 10 November 1920, alongside a Federal Council representing the Länder.</p><p>Its democratic authority came from universal, equal, direct, personal, free, and secret election under proportional representation. The original chamber had 183 seats; later legislatures had 165.</p>"},
      {id:"powers",title:"Legislative and governmental powers",html:"<p>The National Council passed federal statutes, budgets, taxation, treaties requiring legislation, and rules for administration. Governments required parliamentary support and could be removed through a vote of no confidence.</p><p>The chamber met with the Federal Council as the Federal Assembly for defined constitutional purposes. In ordinary lawmaking, the National Council was the politically dominant body.</p>"},
      {id:"presidents",title:"Presidents and parliamentary procedure",html:"<p>A first, second, and third president directed sittings, enforced the standing orders, and could vote as deputies. The distribution of the chairs among parliamentary groups made procedure part of the political balance.</p><p>This arrangement became decisive in March 1933, when each president resigned the chair temporarily to cast a vote and no presiding officer remained to certify the result or continue the sitting.</p>"},
      {id:"composition",title:"Four legislative periods",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Election</th><th>Seats</th><th>Largest group</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td><a href='1920-austrian-legislative-election.html'>17 October 1920</a></td><td>183</td><td>Christian Social · 85</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>21 October 1923</td><td>165</td><td>Christian Social · 82</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>24 April 1927</td><td>165</td><td>Christian Social · 73</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td><a href='1930-austrian-legislative-election.html'>9 November 1930</a></td><td>165</td><td>Social Democratic · 72</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Seventeen governments under nine chancellors served between the first sitting and March 1933. Changes of cabinet did not always require a new election.</p>"},
      {id:"camps",title:"Parliamentary bargaining and political camps",html:"<p>Christian Social, Social Democratic, German-national, agrarian, and later Heimatblock deputies represented broader social camps with newspapers, unions, churches, cooperatives, militias, and provincial governments.</p><p>The chamber nevertheless produced constitutional agreement, monetary stabilization, social law, budgets, and the 1929 constitutional amendment. Street conflict and parliamentary legislation coexisted rather than one simply replacing the other before 1933.</p>"},
      {id:"march",title:"The crisis of March 1933",html:"<p>A disputed railway-strike vote on 4 March led Karl Renner and the other two presidents to resign their chairs in sequence so each could vote. The resignations were procedural acts and did not dissolve the chamber.</p><p>Dollfuss declared that Parliament had eliminated itself. When deputies attempted to reconvene on 15 March, police barred entry and the government began ruling under a wartime economic enabling law.</p>"},
      {id:"rump",title:"Rump session and abolition",html:"<p>After the <a href='austrian-civil-war.html'>February 1934 conflict</a> and prohibition of Social Democracy, the government summoned a controlled remainder of the chamber. It authorized measures used to enact the corporate constitution.</p><p>The <a href='federal-state-of-austria.html'>Federal State</a> replaced elected party parliament with councils and corporative bodies lacking the National Council’s democratic authority.</p>"},
      {id:"memory",title:"An extinguished legislature",html:"<p>Greater German history depicts the National Council as an arena of sterile party conflict ended by national incorporation. Surviving records show a legislature that was polarized and unstable but still capable of binding law and peaceful alternation in parliamentary strength.</p><p>Its destruction required police, emergency law, party prohibition, and constitutional replacement. It did not expire automatically when compromise became difficult.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"federal-constitutional-law-1920.html",kicker:"Creating constitution",label:"Federal Constitutional Law"},{href:"1919-austrian-constituent-assembly-election.html",kicker:"Predecessor assembly",label:"Constituent Assembly"},{href:"1920-austrian-legislative-election.html",kicker:"First election",label:"1920 Legislative Election"},{href:"1930-austrian-legislative-election.html",kicker:"Final election",label:"1930 Legislative Election"},{href:"first-austrian-republic.html",kicker:"Political system",label:"First Austrian Republic"},{href:"federal-state-of-austria.html",kicker:"Authoritarian successor",label:"Federal State of Austria"}],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/fachinfos/rlw/100-Jahre-Nationalrat-und-Bundesrat",label:"Austrian Parliament — one hundred years of the National and Federal Councils"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/recherchieren/statistiken/personen-statistiken/zusammensetzung-nr/index.html",label:"Austrian Parliament — First Republic chamber composition and legislative periods"},{href:"https://www.parlament.gv.at/verstehen/historisches/1918-1945/",label:"Austrian Parliament — the First Republic and end of democracy"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Austria._House_of_Parliament,_Vienna_LCCN2016821598.jpg",label:"Wikimedia Commons — historic Parliament building photograph and rights record"}],
    facts:[["Created","Federal Constitutional Law of 1920"],["First sitting","10 November 1920"],["Election","Universal direct proportional suffrage"],["Seats","183 initially; 165 from 1923"],["Legislative periods","Four"],["Governments during operation","17 under nine chancellors"],["Final free election","9 November 1930"],["Last ordinary sitting","4 March 1933"],["Reconvening blocked","15 March 1933"],["Setting restoration","None"]]
  })
});
